[61.842522, "o", "----- context -----\r\n[1] (source: demo-app/sample-data/pgvector.md#0)\r\n# pgvector\r\n\r\npgvector is a PostgreSQL extension for vector similarity search. It adds the\r\n`vector` data type and distance operators: `<->` (Euclidean), `<#>` (negative\r\ninner product) and `<=>` (cosine distance).\r\n\r\nIt supports exact nearest-neighbour search and two approximate index types:\r\nIVFFlat, which partitions vectors into lists, and HNSW, which builds a\r\nmulti-layer graph and offers a better speed/recall trade-off at the cost of\r\nslower index builds and more memory.\r\n\r\n[2] (source: demo-app/sample-data/rag.md#0)\r\n# Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)\r\n\r\nRetrieval Augmented Generation grounds the answers of a large language model\r\nin a private knowledge base. Documents are split into chunks, each chunk is\r\nturned into a vector by an embedding model, and the vectors are stored in a\r\ndatabase such as PostgreSQL with pgvector.\r\n\r\nWhen a question arrives, it is embedded with the same model, the closest\r\nchunks are retrieved with a similarity search, and they are passed to the\r\nlanguage model as context, together with the instruction to answer only from\r\nthat context.\r\n-------------------\r\n"]
[69.294839, "o", "\r\npgvector supports two approximate index types:\r\n- IVFFlat\r\n- HNSW\r\n\r\nSources:\r\n  [1] demo-app/sample-data/pgvector.md#0  (similarity 0.822)\r\n  [2] demo-app/sample-data/rag.md#0  (similarity 0.636)\r\n"]
